><highlight color="#9643FC" opacity="0.399994" creationdate="D:20250624103155+10'00'" flags="print" IT="HighlightNote" date="D:20250624103200+10'00'" name="d049443e-50fd-459f-a7df-f4bc3a57720a" page="8" coords="45.354004,523.924683,290.555664,523.924683,45.354004,511.109314,290.555664,511.109314,45.354004,512.424622,290.564819,512.424622,45.354004,499.609253,290.564819,499.609253,45.354004,500.924622,290.527771,500.924622,45.354004,488.109253,290.527771,488.109253,45.354004,489.424622,290.573242,489.424622,45.354004,476.609253,290.573242,476.609253,45.354004,477.924622,290.536713,477.924622,45.354004,465.109253,290.536713,465.109253,45.354004,466.424622,290.552124,466.424622,45.354004,453.609253,290.552124,453.609253,45.354004,454.924622,290.559784,454.924622,45.354004,442.109253,290.559784,442.109253,45.354004,443.424622,290.527954,443.424622,45.354004,430.609253,290.527954,430.609253,45.354004,431.924622,290.555237,431.924622,45.354004,419.109253,290.555237,419.109253,45.354004,420.424622,290.539978,420.424622,45.354004,407.609253,290.539978,407.609253,45.354004,408.924622,122.872406,408.924622,45.354004,396.109253,122.872406,396.109253" rect="44.953522,395.708771,290.973724,524.325195" subject="Comment on Text" title="alexd"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#211D1E;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>The free Achilles tendon of trained elite/sub-elite middle-distance runners is stiffer than healthy controls primarily due to an altered geometry. In the absence of group differences in tendon material properties, the larger free Achilles tendon CSA in runners compared to healthy controls appears to be the primary mechanism that protects the free Achilles tendon of runners against high stresses and strains. Although T2</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-7.3pt;text-align:left;color:#211D1E;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>* </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#211D1E;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>relaxation time is sufficiently sensitive to detect differences between trained runners and healthy controls, T2</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-7.3pt;text-align:left;color:#211D1E;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>* </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#211D1E;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>relaxation time should not be considered a valid proxy for Achilles tendon material properties.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="8" rect="595.276001,431.924683,779.276001,523.924683"
/></highlight
